[]{#sec:nuclear-configuration label="sec:nuclear-configuration"}

Nuclei are complicated many-body systems of fermions, whose structure and dynamics emerge from individual interactions among the constituent protons and neutrons. To a remarkably large extent, the latter can be modeled by the non-relativistic Hamiltonian $$H=\sum_{i}\frac{{\bf p}_i^2}{2m_N}+\sum_{i<j} v_{ij}+ \sum_{i<j<k}V_{ijk}\,,
\label{NMBT:ham}$$ where ${\bf p}_i$ denotes the momentum of the $i$-th nucleon with mass $m_N$, while $v_{ij}$ and $V_{ijk}$ are the nucleon-nucleon ($N\!N$) and three-nucleon ($3N$) potentials, resp

To check if an interaction between nucleons occurs, an accept-reject test is performed on the closest nucleon according to a probability distribution $P(b)$ (see e.g. Ref. [@Sjostrand:1987su] for similar considerations) where $b$ is the impact parameter. We impose two conditions on this probability, $$\label{eq:int_prob_conds}
    P(0)=1\quad\textrm{and}\quad
    \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\infty d\varphi \,b db P(b) = \sigma,$$ where the cross section $\sigma$ depends on the incoming particle content and the center-of-mass energy, which is sampled from the nuclear configuration. The second condition

